For the last position in the list, assert that the last index of X is less than or equal to the count of the original array list.

However, I should note that your code needs to be analyzed for any particular problem domain for these assertion statements to make meaning. For example, if you're writing a method to add elements to an ArrayList, it's common that the assert statement be something like "The last index of an added element must be equal to count minus one".

So, the output may vary and the analysis of the code should make the assert statement more specific to make sense of the comments. 

For this example, I've provided you an example based on the code and your comments related to the comment "last position of x in new array is lesser than or equal to old array size".--------------------
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

public static class ArrayList
{
    public static int Add(int x)
    {
        return arrList.Add(x);
    }

    public static void Remove(int x)
    {
        arrList.Remove(x);
    }

    public static void Insert(int index, int x)
    {
        arrList.Insert(index, x);
    }

    public static int this[int index]
    {
        get { return (int)arrList[index]; }
        set { arrList[index] = value; }
    }

    public static int IndexOf(int x)
    {
        return arrList.IndexOf(x);
    }

    public static int LastIndexOf(int x)
    {
        return arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    }

    public static int Count
    {
        get { return arrList.Count; }
    }

    public static bool Contains(int x)
    {
        return arrList.Contains(x);
    }
}

